 { Open files are extremely important in chess as both positional and dynamic tools. In this course so far, we have focused heavily on the pawns and on the minor pieces since in my view it is the ideas around these topics that will serve you best when developing your understanding of positional play. Nevertheless, it would not be wise to ignore the value of open files as these are the channel through which the heavy pieces can spring to life and invade the enemy position. While the queen can benefit from diagonals just as much as she benefits from open files, the rooks rely almost exclusively on open files to enter the action. In this chapter we will focus on the following topics: 1. The value of open files as a means to invade the enemy position and to paralyse the opponent's army. 2. The fight for open files, with a focus on controlling key squares and leveraging outposts to gain the advantage. 3. The power of semi-open files, with a focus on their effect on the pawn structure. Let's begin our discussion with an introductory example taken from the game Tartakower, S - Capablanca, J (1914) . Black has better placed pieces with a powerful central knight and his rooks stacked on the d-file. White for his part may consider opening up the c-file in the future, to activate his rook on c1. One direction the game could be taken in by Capablanca would be to push the d-pawn and rely on the passed pawn. Capablanca, however, preferred simpler solutions where possible, and here he leverages simple tactics to establish a rook on the opponent's 7th rank - a common result of dominating an open file. } 22... { -KEY- } dxc4 $1 23. Qxc4 Qxc4 $1 { A well-timed queen exchange, profiting from the fact that the positionally desirable capture with 24.Rxc4 is not possible due to 24...Nd2 winning the exchange. } 24. bxc4 c6 $1 { Pushing the knight to a more passive square and defending the a7 pawn. } 25. Na3 $2 { The knight is unstable and passive on a3. } ( 25. Nc3 $1 { Challenging the powerful knight would have been the lesser evil although Black would still keep the better chances after } Nc5 $17 ) 
Rd2 $1 { Black achieves his positional goal of entering along the seventh rank, with the a2 pawn immediately under fire. } 26. f3 Nc5 $1 { The knight is perfectly placed here, sheltered by White's c4 pawn. } ( 26... Rxa2 { would have spoiled the advantage and led to an equal endgame after } 27. fxe4 Rxa3 28. Rxf5 $11 ) 
27. Rc2 R8d3 { The second rook invades the White half of the board. } ( 27... Kf7 $1 { Improving the king immediately was even better. } 28. Rxd2 Rxd2 29. Rf2 { If White can exchange both rooks he would have good drawing chances, but Black is not forced to cooperate. } Rd1 30. Rf1 Rd3 $1 31. Nb1 Kf6 { Black's more active rook makes it difficult for White to activate his pieces, while the threat of ...Ke5-d4 is not possible to stop. } 32. Re1 Rd4 $1 { The active rook can easily target White's vulnerable pawns. } 33. Rc1 { If } ( 32... R8d3 32. Na3 $2 Rd2 { forces the extremely passive Ra1 in order to keep the a2 pawn alive. } ) 
Ke5 34. Kf1 Rd3 $19 { The king will reach d4 soon, and once this happens it will be impossible for White to save his queenside pawns. } ) 
28. Re1 f4 29. Kf1 Kf7 30. Re5 $4 { The piece pressure leads to a fatal mistake. Now Black has a winning tactic. } ( 30. Rxd2 $1 { was White's last hope, when he would have kept good chances of holding after } Rxd2 31. Re2 Rd4 $15 { Black still has some pressure, but it would not have been easy to infiltrate with the king. } ) 
Rd1+ $1 31. Ke2 Ne6 $1 { The rooks are so effective that this quiet knight retreat ends the game. White is helpless against the threat of ...Nd4+ followed by ...Rxa3. } 32. Rxe6 Kxe6 { Black was up a full exchange, and went on to win the game. In this first example we saw several of the benefits of open files, from rook activation to control of key squares and threats against all of White's pieces. The doubling of rooks on the file was especially problematic for White to fight against. Let's now turn to our first section, where we'll speak of the value of open files in more detail. } 
